.TH OPTIM 2
.SH NAME
optim: root, newton, bracket, minimise1, minimise, fit, linprog \- root finding, minimisation, curve fitting and linear programming
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "optim.m";
optim := load Optim Optim->PATH;
Result: import optim;

optim->init();
(x, err) := optim->root(f, a, b, tol);
(a, b, err) = optim->bracket(f, a, b);
(x, fx, err) := optim->minimise1(f, a, b, tol);
(r, err) := optim->minimise(f, start, step, tol, maxit);
(r, err) = optim->fit(model, xs, ys, start, tol, maxit);
(r, err) = optim->linprog(c, a, b);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Optim
searches: for where a function crosses zero, for where it is least, for
the parameters that make a model match data, and for the best point of a
linear programme.
.PP
The objective is a function reference, not a table, which is the only
shape that works for a thing being searched \- the search decides where
to look next.
Limbo has no closures, so a caller needing extra data puts it in a
variable of its own module and reads it inside the function, the same
arrangement
.IR krylov (2)
uses.
.SS Roots
.B Root
is Brent's method: bisection so that it cannot fail, with secant and
inverse quadratic interpolation so that it is not slow.
.I A
and
.I b
must straddle a root, which is checked and refused if not \- there is
nothing else honest to do, since nothing says a root is there.
It reaches 1e\-15 on
.IR x \u\s-22\s+2\d\-2
in ten function calls where bisection would need about fifty.
.PP
.B Bracket
widens an interval until it does straddle a root, so that
.B root
has something to work with.
.PP
.B Newton
is the unsafeguarded method, here because it is what a course teaches
and because being able to show its failures is worth something.
It returns a diagnostic when the slope goes to zero, when the iteration
leaves the real numbers, or when it runs out of steps.
On atan started from 2, it is thrown to 7e168 and then divides by a
slope that has underflowed to zero;
.B root
finds the same function's root without difficulty.
.SS Minimisation
.B Minimise1
is Brent's method in one variable: golden section with parabolic
interpolation where that helps.
The interval must contain the minimum; nothing can tell whether it does,
and if it does not the answer is an end of it.
.PP
.B Minimise
is Nelder and Mead's simplex in several variables, which needs no
derivative at all \- the thing to reach for when the function is
measured rather than written down.
A simplex of
.IR n +1
points crawls downhill by reflecting its worst point through the
opposite face, stretching when that helps, pulling in when it does not,
and shrinking onto its best point when nothing does.
That last step is not an optimisation: without it the method reaches the
neighbourhood of a minimum and then crawls forever.
.SS Curve fitting
.B Fit
adjusts a model's parameters to match data by least squares, using
Levenberg-Marquardt: Gauss-Newton where that works and gradient descent
where it does not, sliding between them by a damping parameter that
grows when a step makes things worse and shrinks when it helps.
Derivatives are taken numerically, so a caller supplies only the model.
.PP
This is the nonlinear counterpart of
.IR stats (2)'s
.BR linefit ,
and unlike it, it is
.BR local :
it finds a minimum, not the minimum.
From a bad start on an oscillating model it settles into a different one
and reports, correctly, that it converged.
A caller who needs the global optimum must start near it, or try several
starts and compare.
.SS Linear programming
.B Linprog
maximises
.IR c . x
subject to
.IR ax " <= " b
and
.IR x " >= 0,"
by the simplex method.
Only
.B <=
constraints with a non-negative right-hand side, which is the form every
textbook problem is in and which needs no first phase; anything else is
refused rather than silently mangled.
.PP
The entering column is chosen by
.BR Bland 's
.BR rule ,
which is slower than taking the steepest column and cannot cycle.
That is not a theoretical worry: on Beale's example the steepest rule
returns to a tableau it has already seen and never terminates, and
.IR optimtest (1)
checks that this one does.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/optim.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR stats (2),
.IR linalg (2),
.IR krylov (2),
.IR optimtest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All of these return a diagnostic string, nil on success: a bracket that
does not bracket, an interval that cannot be widened to one, fewer data
points than parameters, a negative right-hand side, an objective nothing
bounds.
.SH BUGS
.B Minimise
and
.B fit
are local.
Neither has any way to tell a local minimum from the global one, and
neither tries.
.PP
There are no constraints in
.B minimise
or
.BR fit :
no bounds on a parameter, no way to hold one fixed.
A parameter that must stay positive has to be reparameterised by the
caller.
.PP
.B Fit
returns the parameters and the sum of squares but no uncertainties on
them.
The matrix needed for that is formed and then thrown away.
.PP
.B Linprog
handles no equality constraints, no variables free in sign, and no
integer restriction, so it cannot express scheduling or assignment
problems at all.
.PP
The jacobian in
.B fit
uses central differences, which is second order in the step rather than
first.
On every problem in
.IR optimtest (1)
a one-sided difference reaches the same answer, and that is not an
accident: where a model fits exactly, the minimum is where the residuals
vanish, and an inaccurate jacobian changes the route and not the
destination.
So nothing here distinguishes the two.
